# =============================================================
# uploads/ - serve files, never execute them
# =============================================================
# Everything under here is attacker-influenced by definition: it is
# whatever users and admins uploaded. api/profile.php already refuses
# anything that is not a real JPEG/PNG/WebP by sniffing the MIME
# server-side, but admin/add-product.php still takes the stored
# extension straight from the client-supplied filename with no check,
# so a .php can reach this directory by that route.
#
# This file is the backstop: even if a script lands here, Apache must
# hand it back as bytes rather than run it. It lives in uploads/ (owned
# by the normal user, mode 644) rather than in avatars/, because
# avatars/ has to be world-writable for Apache to save into it — and a
# protection sitting inside a world-writable directory can be replaced
# by anything that can write there.
# =============================================================

# Neutralise every handler that could execute. RemoveHandler alone is
# not enough: a mod_php AddType mapping would still fire.
<FilesMatch "\.(php|php3|php4|php5|php7|php8|phtml|phar|pl|py|cgi|sh|shtml)$">
    Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

RemoveHandler .php .php3 .php4 .php5 .php7 .php8 .phtml .phar
RemoveType .php .php3 .php4 .php5 .php7 .php8 .phtml .phar
AddType text/plain .php .php3 .php4 .php5 .php7 .php8 .phtml .phar

php_flag engine off

# This Apache build's mime.types predates WebP, so .webp was served with no
# Content-Type at all. The uploader accepts WebP, so declare it.
AddType image/webp .webp

# Nothing here should ever be interpreted as a directory listing either.
Options -Indexes -ExecCGI